avcodec/dca_xll: decode the height channels of the DTS:X standard presentation

A DTS:X stream in the standard presentation is DTS-HD Master Audio with a 7.1
bed and an extension at the end of every XLL frame, behind the syncword the
decoder already recognises (DCA_SYNCWORD_XLL_X). The extension is a 22-byte
header followed by a regular XLL channel set: four channels, every one coded
in full (no residual against the core), no speaker mask, its own NAVI table
with the frame's segment geometry, then a two-byte trailer. Its channels are
the front and rear height pairs, and the encoder folds each of them into the
bed speaker below it (L, R, Lsr, Rsr) at -3 dB, gain code 23170/32768, so the
7.1 bed plays them on players that ignore the extension.

With the new "x_heights" option the decoder parses that channel set with the
existing channel set code, outputs 7.1.4 (TFL, TFR, TBL, TBR), and removes
the fold from the bed at output resolution. The option is off by default;
without it, and for every stream without the extension, the output is
unchanged. A frame whose extension does not decode keeps the 7.1.4 layout
with silent heights and the bed as coded, and the frame still ends where it
did before. The alternate presentations, which carry objects behind
DCA_SYNCWORD_XLL_X_IMAX, are not touched.

The format facts (header size, channel order, the fold) come from the public
analysis in harletty-bridge's dca crate and kristofferR's SpatialAudioBridge;
this is an implementation on FFmpeg's own XLL code, not a translation.

